# Phase 004 causal epistemics rules

Status: **Phase 004 complete — REF-001–REF-030 accepted. Phase 005 next and not started.**

Preserve:

- causal candidate/proposal ≠ support ≠ confirmation;
- leading hypothesis ≠ confirmed cause;
- unresolved ≠ unevaluated/proposed;
- rejected ≠ unsupported/lower ranked;
- causal contribution ≠ primary cause ≠ percentage attribution;
- one supported contributor ≠ exclusion of compatible contributors;
- Investigation closure ≠ confirmation;
- confirmation evidence sufficiency ≠ confirmation authority;
- human title/role ≠ automatic confirmation authority;
- automated reasoning capability ≠ automatic confirmation authority;
- current confirmed status ≠ immutable historical truth.

Rules:

- Bind every material Causal Claim to proposed cause, defined outcome, causal role, subject/context/time, and material mechanism/transmission assumptions.
- Use `proposed`, `supported`, `weakened`, `unresolved`, `rejected`, `confirmed`.
- Treat transitions as non-linear and historical; preserve material revisions with knowledge/evaluation time.
- `Supported` requires applicable evidence that materially supports the proposition below confirmation.
- `Unresolved` means substantive evaluation occurred but evidence remains insufficient, conflicting, non-discriminating, unavailable, or restricted.
- `Rejected` requires sufficient contradiction/exclusion evidence under the applicable standard; lack of support alone is insufficient.
- Evaluate applicable cause/effect occurrence, temporal ordering, historical relationship/mechanism, encounter/transmission, semantic direction, contrasts/interventions, material alternatives, and negative/exclusion coverage.
- Do not create a universal causal confidence score.
- Maintain a bounded material alternative set appropriate to Investigation scope.
- Compatible contributors may coexist; never force one root cause.
- `Primary` requires comparative evidence; never infer it from first-observed location, upstream depth, severity, recency, or organizational preference.
- Qualitative causal roles do not imply percentage attribution.
- `Confirmed` requires an explicit claim-class profile/standard plus bound proposition, sufficient cause/effect evidence, required ordering/mechanism/transmission conditions, material contradiction/alternative review, adequate exclusion coverage, named/versioned standard, resolved confirmation authority/capability, and provenance-bearing confirmation action.
- Phase 005 may refine who/what has confirmation authority, but must not weaken this evidence meaning of `confirmed`.
- Automated confirmation is allowed only if later accepted authority semantics explicitly permit it and the evidence gate is satisfied.
- RCA can mature progressively: candidate/proposed → early supported/weakened/unresolved → deeper investigative RCA → retrospective/confirmation review.
- Speed does not inflate status; elapsed time does not create evidence.
- Direct deterministic control evidence may support strong causal results quickly when its actual standard is met, but broader consequences still require applicable alternatives/coverage review.
- Confirmed claims remain challengeable; preserve historical confirmation, actor/process, standard/profile, and evidence cut.
- Explanations must preserve exact causal status and never translate supported/unresolved into confirmed root cause.

Do not begin Phase 005 without explicit user request.
